Z||000032||JSocha||07-23-2001||11:23 AM||||I'm not a cowboy fan by any means.  <P>Cowboy in old west times was slang for "Outlaw" and it goes to prove that those with the 10 Gallon hats today, I would like to grab em and pull em over the shoulders of some of the morons around here with the "I'ls kick the sh*t out of yuse" attitude especially when it is unwarranted.  Just because the can grab a bull by the horns and tie em up doesn't mean sh*t to me.<P>However, some of those pretty young ladies dressed up like cow girls in them tight jeans (you know, where the image on a quarter can be made out through the back pocket), straddling and posting on a horse, yowsah! &lt;wolf whistle&gt;.  Those ladies got great hips, butts and legs that would make an excellant neck tie.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||EdsB52||07-23-2001||01:51 PM||||I agree with Stage 2Gt for the most part. The one omission is that it wasn't only the European settlers, it was our Great-grandfathers. American Indians weren't recognized as US citizens until 1924 and were put in Indian Schools to try to tame their 'animalistic and savage' ways at that time and shortly thereafter. Indians were displaced from their families to break the bonds and give them feelings of separation to enhance the US agenda. Every Phoenician has driven on 'Indian School Road,' but few know what it means. We like to think all the travesties occurred 500 years ago between Christopher Columbus and the Arawak Indians, but forms of it still occur today.<P>I also commend Stage2 GT for taking a lot of crap and not only refraining from counterattacking, but actually making a parody of his own culture in an attempt to ease some adverse remarks. If some of the very people that are attacking him were attacked respectively, they would come unwound. I also think it's ignorant to take one or two percent of the examples and make them representative of the whole, and I think that's to what Stage2 GT was alluding others are doing.<P>I personally can't stand 95% of the Country and Western music I've encountered, but would never defame someone for enjoying what he or she wants as long as it doesn't hurt anyone else. The main problem I have is that country women are amongst the finest in the world and I can’t get a chance to enjoy them! I love their attention to femininity and notice a competition exists between country women to be as effeminate in appearance and behavior that is not realized in other American cultures. We've all seen American women resemble everything from dominance to rush Limbaugh's 'Feminazi,' but many country women seem to avoid this phenomenon and find effeminate attributes the most desirable. I further think female competition in America centers around career and not femininity, but country women are not as affected to it due to their remoteness. Essentially, I would say that many country women don't suffer from gender dysphoria as some city women do.<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Stage-2GT||07-23-2001||07:36 PM|||| Thanks Ed, You have great insight. You hit the nail right on the head. I don't mind taking a little crap for the way I feel. It's only human nature to be combative when two people can't agree. I usually try to stave off human nautre for my own sake. And Yes, I didn't include our grand fathers and such, but not for unselfish reasons. My grandfather on my fathers side was second generation (in America) Irish, my grandparents on my mothers side were naturalized Americans from Spain. So, technicaly, they weren't involved. However, we are al Americans now and must share in the burden of shame for what has been done in the past. We owe it to each other to move this country forward and not stagnate in the past. Bring on your Rap music, I don't care, If country is what you like, I'll sing with you (and I'm better than most on the Radio) if it's Rock and Roll, I'm with you, if you want to relax with a little easy listening or perhaps some Mozart or Beathoven, Let's do it. But please, respect me the same. That's all I ask. <BR>  If I can help you I will, if you can help me I will appriciate it. <P>S2GT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>"If Hate Was Money, I Would Own The World"||||reg||12
Z||000035||Songman||07-23-2001||08:17 PM||||A lot of what has been said is true.. especially the part about country women! And then especially Southern women! But maybe I am a little biased. And since I make my living playing country music I could be a little biased about that too...<P>The one thing that struck me in the last post.. and it is something that I have talked about many times in the past... was the comment about "moving forward and not stagnating in the past".<P>The scary thing about that is that most people with that attitude completely disregard the past. And since we live in a generation where most people under 21 couldn't even tell you who Hitler was or why we should be scared of Nazis, I see a future of possibly having to live through these horrors again.<P>Throughout history there have been attrocities suffered by different groups of people and usually in the name of moving to the future. Which group will it be next?<P>I love my heritage. It is far from perfect, but it is important to me. I honor those that came before me and hopefully learned from their mistake so that I won't have to make them as they did. I do this in my role as a Southern gentleman and also as a country singer. I don't think Pop Country is moving to the future. I think it is raping what was once a real heartfelt style of music for the almighty dollar. The so-called New South has the same problem in my opinion.<P>As always, you don't have to agree with me but at least respect my right to feel as I do. I would never start a thread saying I AM SO SICK OF... any group of people. That is just me. And trust me, there are a few groups that I am sick of. But I respect your right to do it. I just hope that the next group of people that are killed for their beliefs aren't southern white men... and all because we didn't teach our younger generations to respect the past.<P>And this 'burden of shame' for the things done wrong in the past? What's up with that? I feel no shame for the past. I learn from it. I think slavery was an awful thing, but I didn't do it. And the awful truth that is always forgotten is that white men didn't start it. It was started in Africa by Africans enslaving each other, and then selling their brothers to Europeans. And I feel no shame for the Trail of Tears although it was an awful thing. I have some Cherokee blood in me down the line. But I know I didn't do it... I have no shame for the bombs dropped on Japan. It was awful that is for sure. But I didn't do it, and besides, we didn't start it... I could go on and on throughout the history of the US and the world. One of the big problems these days, at least in the US, is that some group always thinks they are owed something! Sorry, that ain't how it is. We could go off on a completely different tangent here but I won't be the one starting it... I just think that 'burden of shame for the past' is a crock. I don't hurt nobody and I don't owe nobody!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
